          "heading": "System boundaries and environmental inventories",
          "content": "## 1.1 System boundaries and environmental inventories\n\nAn environmental system is a deliberately selected control volume, not a property line. A river segment, settling basin, neighbourhood airshed or waste-sorting facility each includes transfers whose boundaries determine the answer. A stock is the amount present at an instant; a flow is an amount transferred per time. Confusing concentration with total mass leads to apparently sensible but physically impossible comparisons.\n\nStart every inventory by writing the pollutant, medium, boundary, volume, time step and units. List every material inflow and outflow; distinguish transformations from physical transport. A first-order loss term is proportional to the remaining inventory; a fixed-rate extraction may instead be controlled by equipment capacity. Record how each observation was estimated and whether the source fluctuates during the calculation.\n\n**Governing relationship and units**\n\n`dM/dt = \u03a3(Q_in C_in) \u2212 \u03a3(Q_out C_out) + generation \u2212 destruction.`\n\n**Worked context**\n\nA mixing tank with volume 800 m\u00b3, flow 200 m\u00b3/day, and an inert tracer entering at 12 mg/L has hydraulic residence time 4 days. At steady state its outlet equals the inlet concentration if there are no other sinks. This is not evidence that harmful contaminants have been destroyed.\n\n**Reasoning exercise.** Using the running case, make a two-column table: available observations and missing evidence. State a plausible parameter range with units, identify one observation capable of disproving the principal assumption, and explain how the engineering action would change if a measured input were doubled. Do not treat the worked illustrative numbers as calibration data.\n\n**Model critique.** Check dimensional consistency before calculation. If data come from different dates, locations, temperature bases or chemical definitions, reconciliation is required before drawing a conclusion. Report the sensitivity of the decision to uncertain inputs and distinguish the physical result from any legal or public-health judgment."
