          "heading": "Contaminant fate in connected compartments",
          "content": "## 1.2 Contaminant fate in connected compartments\n\nPollutants rarely remain in one phase. Dissolved organic chemicals may sorb to sediments, volatilize into the air, biodegrade or move into aquatic organisms. Each pathway has its own time scale. A single first-order half-life is useful only when the underlying environmental conditions remain close to those during calibration.\n\nPartition coefficients describe equilibrium preferences but do not prove equilibrium has been reached. A sudden discharge may travel downstream faster than it partitions into bed sediments. The relevant comparison is between transport time, reaction time and mixing time; a low concentration at one sampling point can result from dilution rather than actual removal.\n\n**Governing relationship and units**\n\n`C(t)=C\u2080 exp(\u2212kt); t\u00bd=ln(2)/k.`\n\n**Worked context**\n\nA laboratory decay coefficient of 0.23 day\u207b\u00b9 gives a three-day half-life. A groundwater plume with oxygen limitation may degrade at a different rate. Compare expected travel time with several plausible half-lives instead of asserting that the chemical will vanish before reaching a receptor.\n\n**Reasoning exercise.** Using the running case, make a two-column table: available observations and missing evidence. State a plausible parameter range with units, identify one observation capable of disproving the principal assumption, and explain how the engineering action would change if a measured input were doubled. Do not treat the worked illustrative numbers as calibration data.\n\n**Model critique.** Check dimensional consistency before calculation. If data come from different dates, locations, temperature bases or chemical definitions, reconciliation is required before drawing a conclusion. Report the sensitivity of the decision to uncertain inputs and distinguish the physical result from any legal or public-health judgment."
