          "content": "## 1.3 Exposure routes and environmental health logic\n\nEnvironmental concentration is a property of a medium; exposure is the contact experienced by a receptor. Ingestion, inhalation and skin contact require different intake parameters. People may spend different proportions of their time near a source, and sensitive groups can differ from the assumed average adult. An impact assessment should not silently translate an ambient reading into a personal dose.\n\nConstruct a source\u2013pathway\u2013receptor diagram before calculating risk. Eliminate impossible pathways by evidence, not convenience. For a school near a dusty construction site, identify particle-size distribution, hours outdoors, building filtration, prevailing winds and sampling height. Identify what evidence would distinguish the construction source from background traffic.\n\n**Governing relationship and units**\n\n`Average daily intake = concentration \u00d7 intake rate \u00d7 exposure frequency \u00d7 duration /(body mass \u00d7 averaging time).`\n\n**Worked context**\n\nFor illustrative water intake, 0.02 mg/L \u00d7 2 L/day \u00f7 70 kg gives 0.000571 mg kg\u207b\u00b9 day\u207b\u00b9 for daily exposure. A hazard quotient cannot be calculated responsibly without a credible, substance-specific reference dose and route.\n\n**Reasoning exercise.** Using the running case, make a two-column table: available observations and missing evidence. State a plausible parameter range with units, identify one observation capable of disproving the principal assumption, and explain how the engineering action would change if a measured input were doubled. Do not treat the worked illustrative numbers as calibration data.\n\n**Model critique.** Check dimensional consistency before calculation. If data come from different dates, locations, temperature bases or chemical definitions, reconciliation is required before drawing a conclusion. Report the sensitivity of the decision to uncertain inputs and distinguish the physical result from any legal or public-health judgment."
