          "heading": "Physical and chemical indicators",
          "content": "## 2.1 Physical and chemical indicators\n\nTurbidity is an optical scattering measurement; it is not a direct mass concentration of microorganisms or sediment. Conductivity is an indicator of dissolved ions, whereas total dissolved solids depend on ion composition and the conversion method used. Dissolved oxygen responds to temperature, salinity, photosynthesis, respiration and reaeration. A parameter can be a useful alarm without being a direct measure of health risk.\n\nField readings require instrument verification, temperature compensation and sample context. A grab sample cannot represent a highly variable storm hydrograph unless a justification is provided. If two laboratories disagree, evaluate filtration, preservation, holding time, blank contamination and analytical method before averaging their values.\n\n**Governing relationship and units**\n\n`Mass load (kg/day)=Q(m\u00b3/day)\u00d7C(mg/L)\u00d70.001.`\n\n**Worked context**\n\nA stream flow of 2,500 m\u00b3/day at 18 mg/L suspended solids carries 45 kg/day. If the discharge doubles while concentration remains constant, the total delivered load doubles even though the laboratory result looks unchanged.\n\n**Reasoning exercise.** Using the running case, make a two-column table: available observations and missing evidence. State a plausible parameter range with units, identify one observation capable of disproving the principal assumption, and explain how the engineering action would change if a measured input were doubled. Do not treat the worked illustrative numbers as calibration data.\n\n**Model critique.** Check dimensional consistency before calculation. If data come from different dates, locations, temperature bases or chemical definitions, reconciliation is required before drawing a conclusion. Report the sensitivity of the decision to uncertain inputs and distinguish the physical result from any legal or public-health judgment."
