          "heading": "Oxygen demand and nutrient cycling",
          "content": "## 2.2 Oxygen demand and nutrient cycling\n\nBiochemical oxygen demand estimates oxygen consumed by microbial oxidation under specified test conditions. Chemical oxygen demand captures a different set of oxidizable substances and generally cannot be converted to BOD using a universal ratio. Nitrification consumes oxygen; phosphorus and nitrogen can accelerate downstream growth when light and ecological conditions permit. Nutrient status is governed by limitation and biological uptake, not concentration alone.\n\nInterpret BOD, COD, ammonia, nitrate and orthophosphate as related but distinct state variables. A measured five-day BOD is not necessarily ultimate demand. In a river reach, deoxygenation competes with reaeration; nighttime respiration and daylight photosynthesis can produce strong daily cycles that a noon grab sample misses.\n\n**Governing relationship and units**\n\n`dD/dt = k_d L(t) \u2212 k_r D(t), where D is oxygen deficit.`\n\n**Worked context**\n\nAt 20\u00b0C, a river with DO saturation near 9 mg/L and measured DO 5 mg/L has a deficit of about 4 mg/L. The saturation value changes with local conditions; a simplified demonstration must not be used as an ecological compliance assessment.\n\n**Reasoning exercise.** Using the running case, make a two-column table: available observations and missing evidence. State a plausible parameter range with units, identify one observation capable of disproving the principal assumption, and explain how the engineering action would change if a measured input were doubled. Do not treat the worked illustrative numbers as calibration data.\n\n**Model critique.** Check dimensional consistency before calculation. If data come from different dates, locations, temperature bases or chemical definitions, reconciliation is required before drawing a conclusion. Report the sensitivity of the decision to uncertain inputs and distinguish the physical result from any legal or public-health judgment."
