          "content": "## 2.3 Microbial quality and sampling designs\n\nIndicator organisms such as E. coli can signal fecal contamination but do not measure every pathogen or infective dose. A sample with no detected organisms is not evidence that the supply is sterile. The probability of missing a rare contamination event depends on sample size, timing and detection limits. Chlorine residual alone is also insufficient evidence of microbiological safety.\n\nSeparate the sampling objective into surveillance, process control, incident response and source characterization. For a distribution network, choose points representing storage, hydraulic age, pressure disturbances and vulnerable endpoints. Include field blanks, duplicate samples, chain-of-custody records and immediate response criteria agreed with the responsible authority.\n\n**Governing relationship and units**\n\n`Percent positive = positive samples / total valid samples \u00d7100%.`\n\n**Worked context**\n\nA surveillance plan with 2 positive results among 40 valid tests reports 5% positive, but the uncertainty is large. If the two positives followed a pipe repair, investigating pressure loss is more useful than treating all samples as independent random observations.\n\n**Reasoning exercise.** Using the running case, make a two-column table: available observations and missing evidence. State a plausible parameter range with units, identify one observation capable of disproving the principal assumption, and explain how the engineering action would change if a measured input were doubled. Do not treat the worked illustrative numbers as calibration data.\n\n**Model critique.** Check dimensional consistency before calculation. If data come from different dates, locations, temperature bases or chemical definitions, reconciliation is required before drawing a conclusion. Report the sensitivity of the decision to uncertain inputs and distinguish the physical result from any legal or public-health judgment."
