          "secNumber": "2.6",
          "heading": "Hydrochemistry and transport in receiving waters",
          "content": "## 2.4 Hydrochemistry and transport in receiving waters\n\npH controls carbonate equilibria, metal solubility and the proportion of ammonia present as un-ionized NH\u2083. Alkalinity describes acid-neutralizing capacity, not simply the pH value. Mixing of two different-quality streams requires flow-weighted concentrations when the constituent behaves conservatively, while reactions and settling demand additional terms.\n\nRecord whether concentrations are expressed as an element, ion or compound and track equivalents when working with acid\u2013base balance. For a mixing zone, calculate both flows on a consistent time basis and evaluate whether complete mixing is physically plausible at the location where a compliance sample is collected.\n\n**Governing relationship and units**\n\n`C_mix=(Q\u2081C\u2081+Q\u2082C\u2082)/(Q\u2081+Q\u2082).`\n\n**Worked context**\n\nA river at 3 m\u00b3/s and 2 mg/L conservatively mixes with an effluent at 0.5 m\u00b3/s and 14 mg/L to give about 3.71 mg/L after complete mixing. The concentration close to the outfall may be higher because lateral dispersion takes time.\n\n**Reasoning exercise.** Using the running case, make a two-column table: available observations and missing evidence. State a plausible parameter range with units, identify one observation capable of disproving the principal assumption, and explain how the engineering action would change if a measured input were doubled. Do not treat the worked illustrative numbers as calibration data.\n\n**Model critique.** Check dimensional consistency before calculation. If data come from different dates, locations, temperature bases or chemical definitions, reconciliation is required before drawing a conclusion. Report the sensitivity of the decision to uncertain inputs and distinguish the physical result from any legal or public-health judgment.\n\n\n### Synthesis and field decision\n\nIn the case scenario, design a sampling plan with critical control points, detection limits, duplicates and an action matrix. For each quantity used, identify its spatial representativeness, time basis, uncertainty and the responsible person who can confirm the value. Explicitly document how the plan changes during equipment failure or extreme conditions.\n\n\n### Unit assessment\n\n1. For water quality: measurements, sampling and data quality, define a control volume and list all stocks, transports and reaction terms with units.\n2. Explain how different sampling times or locations could reverse the conclusion in this scenario: A municipal intake downstream of intermittent industrial discharges needs defensible monitoring, not only a list of laboratory parameters.\n3. Choose two uncertainties and quantify which has the larger effect on the recommendation. Show a reproducible parameter table.\n4. Use one of the four governing relationships to calculate a low, central and high case, then explain why a numerical result is not a permit limit.\n5. Design an independent field or bench experiment that discriminates between a transport limitation and a reaction limitation.\n6. Write a corrective-action plan for equipment failure or a surprising monitoring result; identify response time and independent verification.\n7. Construct a single-page public explanation of the issue without concealing unresolved uncertainty.\n8. Critique a proposal that reports percent removal but omits residual streams, peak-flow capacity and monitoring quality.\n\n**Assessment policy:** A numerical answer without units, documented assumptions, and interpretation is incomplete. Explain what additional observation or regulation would be needed to authorize real-world action."
