          "secNumber": "3.3",
          "heading": "Coagulation and flocculation process selection",
          "content": "## 3.1 Coagulation and flocculation process selection\n\nColloids remain suspended because of particle size and surface electrochemistry. Coagulation changes stability, while flocculation promotes collisions and growth of settleable aggregates. Rapid mixing, chemical dose, pH, alkalinity and temperature interact. More coagulant does not always improve removal; overdosing can destabilize flocs, raise dissolved residuals or increase sludge costs.\n\nJar tests identify a useful operating window rather than a universal best dose. Compare clarified turbidity, filterability, pH change and residual metal with an untreated control. The engineer should avoid transferring a laboratory optimum directly to a full-scale plant without accounting for mixing energy, detention distribution and changing raw-water quality.\n\n**Governing relationship and units**\n\n`Approximate mean velocity gradient G = \u221a(P/(\u03bcV)).`\n\n**Worked context**\n\nAn operator increases alum dose to control a monsoon turbidity spike. The treated pH falls unexpectedly. Before further dosing, check raw-water alkalinity, actual chemical strength, pump calibration and sludge blanket performance. The immediate goal is stable barrier operation.\n\n**Reasoning exercise.** Using the running case, make a two-column table: available observations and missing evidence. State a plausible parameter range with units, identify one observation capable of disproving the principal assumption, and explain how the engineering action would change if a measured input were doubled. Do not treat the worked illustrative numbers as calibration data.\n\n**Model critique.** Check dimensional consistency before calculation. If data come from different dates, locations, temperature bases or chemical definitions, reconciliation is required before drawing a conclusion. Report the sensitivity of the decision to uncertain inputs and distinguish the physical result from any legal or public-health judgment."
