          "secNumber": "3.4",
          "heading": "Sedimentation and filtration hydraulics",
          "content": "## 3.2 Sedimentation and filtration hydraulics\n\nIn ideal discrete-particle sedimentation, a particle settles if its terminal velocity exceeds basin surface overflow rate. Real clarifiers deviate from this simple comparison because of density currents, floc growth, wind, sludge disturbance and short-circuiting. Filtration provides a different barrier: particles are captured within media or at surfaces, and head loss rises as solids accumulate.\n\nDesign reasoning starts with particle-size distribution and hydraulic loading, then checks residence time, solids storage, sludge withdrawal and peak flow. A filter can produce visually clear water while a bypass, seal failure or poorly managed backwash compromises the microbial barrier. Backwash timing and ripening after a run must be considered alongside pressure losses.\n\n**Governing relationship and units**\n\n`Surface loading v\u2080=Q/A; Darcy head loss h=\u03bcLv/(\u03c1gk).`\n\n**Worked context**\n\nA clarifier carrying 4,000 m\u00b3/day over 200 m\u00b2 has overflow velocity 20 m/day. Ideal particles settling at 30 m/day may be captured under assumed flow; particles at 5 m/day are not automatically captured by the same unit.\n\n**Reasoning exercise.** Using the running case, make a two-column table: available observations and missing evidence. State a plausible parameter range with units, identify one observation capable of disproving the principal assumption, and explain how the engineering action would change if a measured input were doubled. Do not treat the worked illustrative numbers as calibration data.\n\n**Model critique.** Check dimensional consistency before calculation. If data come from different dates, locations, temperature bases or chemical definitions, reconciliation is required before drawing a conclusion. Report the sensitivity of the decision to uncertain inputs and distinguish the physical result from any legal or public-health judgment."
