          "content": "## 3.4 Process trains, failures and water safety plans\n\nNo unit process should be judged solely by its average removal percentage. A water treatment train depends on combinations of source protection, particle removal, disinfection, pressure management and monitoring. If the source contains dissolved salts, ordinary clarification and sand filtration cannot reliably remove them; membrane processes or other separation approaches may be required, with reject-stream management.\n\nConstruct a hazard table with triggering event, early warning, preventive control, monitoring, corrective action and responsible person. Test plausible incidents: chemical stock-out, coagulant dosing fault, rapid turbidity increase, power interruption and cross-connection. A water safety plan is a living operating method rather than a once-off compliance document.\n\n**Governing relationship and units**\n\n`C_out=C_in\u220f(1\u2212\u03b7_i) is only justified if removal fractions are applicable, independent and defined for the same constituent.`\n\n**Worked context**\n\nA pre-filter barrier removes 80% of particulate mass and a downstream filter removes 90% of the remaining mass, producing 98% total mass removal in an ideal two-stage calculation. That does not mean 98% pathogen protection.\n\n**Reasoning exercise.** Using the running case, make a two-column table: available observations and missing evidence. State a plausible parameter range with units, identify one observation capable of disproving the principal assumption, and explain how the engineering action would change if a measured input were doubled. Do not treat the worked illustrative numbers as calibration data.\n\n**Model critique.** Check dimensional consistency before calculation. If data come from different dates, locations, temperature bases or chemical definitions, reconciliation is required before drawing a conclusion. Report the sensitivity of the decision to uncertain inputs and distinguish the physical result from any legal or public-health judgment.\n\n\n### Synthesis and field decision\n\nIn the case scenario, compare two treatment trains, perform a jar-test decision exercise and write an incident response sequence. For each quantity used, identify its spatial representativeness, time basis, uncertainty and the responsible person who can confirm the value. Explicitly document how the plan changes during equipment failure or extreme conditions.\n\n\n### Unit assessment\n\n1. For water treatment: separation, barriers and disinfection, define a control volume and list all stocks, transports and reaction terms with units.\n2. Explain how different sampling times or locations could reverse the conclusion in this scenario: An intake experiences sudden storm turbidity, pesticide detections and low alkalinity during the wet season.\n3. Choose two uncertainties and quantify which has the larger effect on the recommendation. Show a reproducible parameter table.\n4. Use one of the four governing relationships to calculate a low, central and high case, then explain why a numerical result is not a permit limit.\n5. Design an independent field or bench experiment that discriminates between a transport limitation and a reaction limitation.\n6. Write a corrective-action plan for equipment failure or a surprising monitoring result; identify response time and independent verification.\n7. Construct a single-page public explanation of the issue without concealing unresolved uncertainty.\n8. Critique a proposal that reports percent removal but omits residual streams, peak-flow capacity and monitoring quality.\n\n**Assessment policy:** A numerical answer without units, documented assumptions, and interpretation is incomplete. Explain what additional observation or regulation would be needed to authorize real-world action."
