          "id": "sec-4-3",
          "secNumber": "4.3",
          "heading": "Wastewater characterization and preliminary treatment",
          "content": "## 4.1 Wastewater characterization and preliminary treatment\n\nSanitary wastewater varies over a day and can receive wet-weather infiltration, industrial discharges or shock loads. Flow equalization can protect downstream biological processes, but it does not remove the total mass of pollutants. Screens and grit chambers protect pumps and downstream equipment; they are not substitutes for primary sedimentation or biological conversion.\n\nBuild a flow and load profile using hourly data, not only an average concentration. Evaluate BOD, COD, soluble and particulate fractions, total suspended solids, nitrogen forms, phosphorus, pH, alkalinity and potential toxicants. For industrial inputs, check inhibition and chemical compatibility before assuming that additional aeration will solve every performance problem.\n\n**Governing relationship and units**\n\n`L_BOD (kg/day)=Q(m\u00b3/day)\u00d7S(mg/L)\u00d70.001.`\n\n**Worked context**\n\nAt 12,000 m\u00b3/day and 220 mg/L BOD, the influent load is 2,640 kg/day. Doubling flow with rainwater while halving BOD concentration gives the same computed load, but different hydraulics may still overload clarifiers.\n\n**Reasoning exercise.** Using the running case, make a two-column table: available observations and missing evidence. State a plausible parameter range with units, identify one observation capable of disproving the principal assumption, and explain how the engineering action would change if a measured input were doubled. Do not treat the worked illustrative numbers as calibration data.\n\n**Model critique.** Check dimensional consistency before calculation. If data come from different dates, locations, temperature bases or chemical definitions, reconciliation is required before drawing a conclusion. Report the sensitivity of the decision to uncertain inputs and distinguish the physical result from any legal or public-health judgment."
