          "id": "sec-4-5",
          "secNumber": "4.5",
          "heading": "Nitrogen and phosphorus transformations",
          "content": "## 4.3 Nitrogen and phosphorus transformations\n\nNitrification oxidizes reduced nitrogen and consumes alkalinity and oxygen; denitrification reduces nitrate under anoxic conditions when a suitable electron donor is present. Neither process is captured by simply multiplying total nitrogen by a generic removal efficiency. Biological phosphorus removal depends on cycling anaerobic and aerobic conditions and on carbon availability.\n\nDraw nitrogen species and reaction zones through the treatment train. Distinguish ammonia toxicity, nitrate discharge limits, and total-nitrogen mass balance. A low ammonia effluent may still have high nitrate. Chemical phosphorus precipitation shifts material into sludge; nutrient recovery requires a recovery pathway and quality assurance beyond simply precipitating a solid.\n\n**Governing relationship and units**\n\n`Removed nutrient load (kg/day)=0.001Q(C_in\u2212C_out).`\n\n**Worked context**\n\nAt 8,000 m\u00b3/day, reducing total phosphorus from 6 to 1 mg/L removes about 40 kg/day from the water stream. The phosphorus remains in biomass, precipitates or another residual stream until final handling.\n\n**Reasoning exercise.** Using the running case, make a two-column table: available observations and missing evidence. State a plausible parameter range with units, identify one observation capable of disproving the principal assumption, and explain how the engineering action would change if a measured input were doubled. Do not treat the worked illustrative numbers as calibration data.\n\n**Model critique.** Check dimensional consistency before calculation. If data come from different dates, locations, temperature bases or chemical definitions, reconciliation is required before drawing a conclusion. Report the sensitivity of the decision to uncertain inputs and distinguish the physical result from any legal or public-health judgment."
