          "id": "sec-5-3",
          "secNumber": "5.3",
          "heading": "Air contaminants and emission inventories",
          "content": "## 5.1 Air contaminants and emission inventories\n\nAmbient air pollution combines primary emissions with secondary pollutants formed through atmospheric chemistry. Particulate matter is defined by aerodynamic size ranges, not by chemical composition; gaseous species behave differently. Stack concentration and ambient concentration are different quantities. A low concentration at a tall stack may still correspond to a large emission mass rate if volumetric flow is high.\n\nConstruct an emission inventory with fuel use, activity, emission factor, capture efficiency and control-system uptime. Separate routine emissions from start-up, maintenance and upset events. Local background, meteorology and topography can dominate the receptor measurement; a source-attribution claim requires data rather than proximity alone.\n\n**Governing relationship and units**\n\n`Emission rate = activity rate \u00d7 emission factor \u00d7 (1\u2212control efficiency).`\n\n**Worked context**\n\nA boiler with 4,000 kg fuel/day and illustrative 2 g/kg particulate factor emits 8 kg/day before control. A tested 95% mass removal would reduce this estimate to 0.4 kg/day; measurement uncertainty and operating conditions still matter.\n\n**Reasoning exercise.** Using the running case, make a two-column table: available observations and missing evidence. State a plausible parameter range with units, identify one observation capable of disproving the principal assumption, and explain how the engineering action would change if a measured input were doubled. Do not treat the worked illustrative numbers as calibration data.\n\n**Model critique.** Check dimensional consistency before calculation. If data come from different dates, locations, temperature bases or chemical definitions, reconciliation is required before drawing a conclusion. Report the sensitivity of the decision to uncertain inputs and distinguish the physical result from any legal or public-health judgment."
