          "content": "## 5.2 Dispersion and source\u2013receptor relationships\n\nAtmospheric transport depends on wind, turbulence, stability, terrain and release characteristics. The Gaussian plume is a conceptual steady-state model under idealized conditions, not an adequate replacement for a regulatory dispersion assessment. Changes in mixing height or stable nighttime conditions can produce counterintuitive ground-level patterns.\n\nWhen using an illustrative plume calculator, state wind direction, receptor height, source elevation, stack exit effects and dispersion parameterization. Evaluate crosswind displacement and how the centerline maximum moves with effective source height. The US EPA AERMOD system uses far more complete meteorological and terrain treatment and evolves through documented revisions.\n\n**Governing relationship and units**\n\n`C(x,0,0)\u2248[Q/(\u03c0u\u03c3_y\u03c3_z)]exp(\u2212H\u00b2/(2\u03c3_z\u00b2)), under idealized reflection and steady wind.`\n\n**Worked context**\n\nA high effective source height can reduce immediate near-field ground concentration but does not destroy emissions. A valley or changing wind regime may invalidate flat-terrain assumptions. Real permits require the jurisdictionally adopted model and data.\n\n**Reasoning exercise.** Using the running case, make a two-column table: available observations and missing evidence. State a plausible parameter range with units, identify one observation capable of disproving the principal assumption, and explain how the engineering action would change if a measured input were doubled. Do not treat the worked illustrative numbers as calibration data.\n\n**Model critique.** Check dimensional consistency before calculation. If data come from different dates, locations, temperature bases or chemical definitions, reconciliation is required before drawing a conclusion. Report the sensitivity of the decision to uncertain inputs and distinguish the physical result from any legal or public-health judgment."
