          "content": "## 5.3 Emission controls and removal verification\n\nCyclones, fabric filters, electrostatic precipitators, absorbers and adsorption beds remove different pollutants through different mechanisms. A cyclone relies on particle inertia and will not reliably remove a soluble gas simply because both pollutants share a stack. Adsorption involves finite capacity and eventual breakthrough; wet scrubbers generate wastewater and sometimes sludge.\n\nDescribe the target species and particle size, upstream temperature, humidity, pressure drop, corrosion risk and expected control availability. Distinguish equipment design removal efficiency from measured whole-process emission reduction. A bypassing filter can meet an equipment catalogue rating while an actual stack test shows poor overall capture.\n\n**Governing relationship and units**\n\n`\u03b7 = 1 \u2212 C_out/C_in for equal volumetric basis, with concentration measurements corrected to the same conditions.`\n\n**Worked context**\n\nAt matched stack flow and reference conditions, an inlet particulate concentration of 150 mg/m\u00b3 and outlet of 7.5 mg/m\u00b3 implies 95% removal. If flows differ, mass rates should be compared instead.\n\n**Reasoning exercise.** Using the running case, make a two-column table: available observations and missing evidence. State a plausible parameter range with units, identify one observation capable of disproving the principal assumption, and explain how the engineering action would change if a measured input were doubled. Do not treat the worked illustrative numbers as calibration data.\n\n**Model critique.** Check dimensional consistency before calculation. If data come from different dates, locations, temperature bases or chemical definitions, reconciliation is required before drawing a conclusion. Report the sensitivity of the decision to uncertain inputs and distinguish the physical result from any legal or public-health judgment."
