          "id": "sec-6-4",
          "secNumber": "6.4",
          "heading": "Recycling quality and materials recovery",
          "content": "## 6.2 Recycling quality and materials recovery\n\nThe presence of recyclable material in mixed waste does not establish an economically viable recovered product. Capture efficiency, contamination, sorting purity, demand, transport distance and occupational safety determine outcomes. Source separation can improve quality but depends on participation, reliable separate collection and actual processing capacity.\n\nTrack input mass, rejected contamination, recovered saleable mass and final destination for each material stream. A high facility diversion claim may be inflated if rejected bales later go to landfill. Compare avoided virgin-material production with sorting, washing and transport impacts using an explicit functional unit and locality-specific emissions data.\n\n**Governing relationship and units**\n\n`Recovered mass = incoming mass \u00d7 target fraction \u00d7 capture rate \u00d7 processing yield.`\n\n**Worked context**\n\nA 100 t/day stream containing 18% recoverable plastic at 70% capture and 85% processing yield produces about 10.71 t/day of recovered plastic, before any later product losses.\n\n**Reasoning exercise.** Using the running case, make a two-column table: available observations and missing evidence. State a plausible parameter range with units, identify one observation capable of disproving the principal assumption, and explain how the engineering action would change if a measured input were doubled. Do not treat the worked illustrative numbers as calibration data.\n\n**Model critique.** Check dimensional consistency before calculation. If data come from different dates, locations, temperature bases or chemical definitions, reconciliation is required before drawing a conclusion. Report the sensitivity of the decision to uncertain inputs and distinguish the physical result from any legal or public-health judgment."
