          "id": "sec-6-5",
          "secNumber": "6.5",
          "heading": "Organics treatment and landfill dynamics",
          "content": "## 6.3 Organics treatment and landfill dynamics\n\nComposting uses managed aerobic decomposition to stabilize organics when moisture, oxygen, porosity and carbon-to-nitrogen balance are suitable. Anaerobic digestion produces biogas in the absence of oxygen but requires feedstock control and digestate management. Landfills produce leachate and gas whose composition and timing depend on waste age, moisture, composition and containment.\n\nA first-order landfill methane model is a screening inventory model; landfill-cover oxidation, collection efficiency, site climate and historical disposal alter actual releases. Decisions about composting and digestion should consider contamination by glass, plastics or metals and whether the end product has a viable, safe use.\n\n**Governing relationship and units**\n\n`Illustrative gas generation G(t)=k L\u2080 M exp(\u2212kt), with unit-consistent mass and gas potential.`\n\n**Worked context**\n\nA legacy deposit can continue producing gas decades after placement. Doubling the assumed first-order decay coefficient increases initial predicted generation but does not double integrated lifetime potential.\n\n**Reasoning exercise.** Using the running case, make a two-column table: available observations and missing evidence. State a plausible parameter range with units, identify one observation capable of disproving the principal assumption, and explain how the engineering action would change if a measured input were doubled. Do not treat the worked illustrative numbers as calibration data.\n\n**Model critique.** Check dimensional consistency before calculation. If data come from different dates, locations, temperature bases or chemical definitions, reconciliation is required before drawing a conclusion. Report the sensitivity of the decision to uncertain inputs and distinguish the physical result from any legal or public-health judgment."
