          "content": "## 7.2 Quantitative exposure and risk screening\n\nHazard identifies inherent ability to cause harm; risk concerns probability and consequence under a defined exposure. Conservative assumptions are appropriate for screening but should be reported rather than concealed. A hazard quotient exceeding one signals a need for more evaluation, not proof that illness will occur; below one is not a universal certificate of safety.\n\nSpecify exposure duration, body mass, intake, absorption, toxicity value, competing routes and sensitive populations. Include a realistic high-exposure scenario and a central scenario. Regulatory toxicity values and acceptable thresholds vary by pollutant and jurisdiction and must come from current authoritative sources.\n\n**Governing relationship and units**\n\n`CDI=(C\u00d7IR\u00d7EF\u00d7ED)/(BW\u00d7AT).`\n\n**Worked context**\n\nAn illustrative drinking-water exposure concentration of 0.01 mg/L, intake 2 L/day and body mass 65 kg gives 3.08\u00d710\u207b\u2074 mg/kg/day for daily exposure. The engineering decision requires compound-specific toxicology and actual sampling.\n\n**Reasoning exercise.** Using the running case, make a two-column table: available observations and missing evidence. State a plausible parameter range with units, identify one observation capable of disproving the principal assumption, and explain how the engineering action would change if a measured input were doubled. Do not treat the worked illustrative numbers as calibration data.\n\n**Model critique.** Check dimensional consistency before calculation. If data come from different dates, locations, temperature bases or chemical definitions, reconciliation is required before drawing a conclusion. Report the sensitivity of the decision to uncertain inputs and distinguish the physical result from any legal or public-health judgment."
