          "content": "## 7.3 Contaminated-site characterization and cleanup\n\nContaminated soil and groundwater are heterogeneous. Sampling near a source can miss preferential flow paths, and a seemingly clean well may simply be screened above the plume. Remediation options include removal, containment, pump-and-treat, soil vapour extraction, bioremediation and monitored natural attenuation, each appropriate only under particular hydrogeology and chemical behaviour.\n\nUse a conceptual site model that connects source history, soil layers, groundwater gradient, measured chemical distribution and receptors. Establish data quality objectives and a staged investigation. Successful cleanup must be judged by validated endpoints and long-term monitoring, not merely the visible removal of contaminated surface soil.\n\n**Governing relationship and units**\n\n`Advective travel time \u2248 distance \u00d7 effective porosity / Darcy flux, for a simplified saturated homogeneous path.`\n\n**Worked context**\n\nA plume that travels 0.2 m/day could cross a 400 m distance in around 2,000 days absent retardation and dispersion. Sorption and biodegradation may slow particular constituents, but unknown preferential pathways can undermine this simple estimate.\n\n**Reasoning exercise.** Using the running case, make a two-column table: available observations and missing evidence. State a plausible parameter range with units, identify one observation capable of disproving the principal assumption, and explain how the engineering action would change if a measured input were doubled. Do not treat the worked illustrative numbers as calibration data.\n\n**Model critique.** Check dimensional consistency before calculation. If data come from different dates, locations, temperature bases or chemical definitions, reconciliation is required before drawing a conclusion. Report the sensitivity of the decision to uncertain inputs and distinguish the physical result from any legal or public-health judgment."
