          "id": "sec-8-23",
          "secNumber": "8.23",
          "heading": "Practice examination: integrated environmental engineering",
          "content": "## Practice examination: integrated environmental engineering\n\n**Problem 1: Water quality and treatment selection.** An intake with 12,000 m\u00b3/day raw water shows elevated solids after storms and intermittent indicators of fecal contamination. Prepare a minimum monitoring programme. Estimate which loading and barrier variables change during a doubled peak flow. Explain why a sedimentation surface-loading calculation alone cannot verify microbiological safety. Your recommendation must separate preventive watershed measures from treatment barriers and emergency response.\n\n**Problem 2: Wastewater loads and resilience.** A town generates 9,000 m\u00b3/day sewage at 250 mg/L BOD, increasing to 24,000 m\u00b3/day at 110 mg/L during wet weather. Compare load and hydraulic stress. Propose the measurements that distinguish infiltration from deliberate industrial discharge. Design an upgrade screening plan that includes clarifier hydraulic capacity, biomass inventory, nutrient objectives, power continuity and residual solids management.\n\n**Problem 3: Air-quality attribution.** A proposed material-processing site is near a school. Form a source inventory and upwind/downwind monitoring experiment. Explain why a centreline Gaussian plume curve cannot independently verify compliance under changing weather and terrain. Specify independent monitoring, control-equipment uptime, worker exposure and the role of current regulatory dispersion procedures.\n\n**Problem 4: Waste logistics and climate.** A community produces 95 t/day municipal waste, 28% organic, 19% plastic. Collection reaches 80% of generation. Evaluate a combined sorting, composting, controlled disposal and energy-recovery option, showing where material streams and carbon emissions go. Compare two options with the same functional unit, include disposal of rejected contamination and discuss the data needed before claiming greenhouse-gas savings.\n\n**Problem 5: Contaminated land and public trust.** An abandoned workshop has shallow groundwater and planned new housing. Propose a phased investigation, screening risk calculation and mitigation alternatives. Explain the difference between measured concentration, normalized intake, toxicity judgment and legally approved cleanup. Describe how to communicate uncertain findings without exposing personal information or falsely assuring residents that every exposure pathway has been resolved.\n\n**Marking rubric (100 points):** physically coherent mass and energy balances (25), correct definitions and units (15), defensible exposure and source analysis (15), alternative and failure mode comparison (15), transparent evidence and uncertainty (15), treatment/waste residual integration (10), clear ethically responsible communication (5).\n\n\n# Field protocols: applied environmental engineering practice"
